<Project>

  <!--
    Imported automatically by NuGet into any project referencing Datra.

    A .targets rather than a .props on purpose: NuGet imports build/<id>.props
    ABOVE the project body and build/<id>.targets below it, and the condition
    here reads PublishTrimmed — which the consuming project has not set yet at
    props time. As a .props this silently did nothing.

    Datra itself is IsTrimmable (see Datra.csproj), so a consumer that never
    calls it has it trimmed away. YamlDotNet is not: it carries no IsTrimmable
    metadata, so ILLink gives it the `copy` action and it stays in the publish
    output even with zero references left pointing at it.

    Opt in when nothing in the application parses YAML. Datra's YAML path is
    reflection-driven, so a trimmed YamlDotNet does not degrade — it throws.
    JSON and CSV are unaffected either way.

      <PropertyGroup>
        <DatraTrimYaml>true</DatraTrimYaml>
      </PropertyGroup>

    Measured on a Blazor WebAssembly consumer: Datra 347 KB and YamlDotNet
    293 KB leave the wire, and the AOT native blob drops 547 KB brotli with them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(DatraTrimYaml)' == 'true' AND '$(PublishTrimmed)' == 'true'">
    <TrimmableAssembly Include="YamlDotNet" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
